A||||9||Rod101||Newbie Questions - various
Z||000000||Rod101||08-05-99||10:25 AM||||I have some questions most newbies might be interested (Well, I am very interested):<p>1) I just realised that the Fiero body is some kind of ... Plastic? Can I used over the counter touchup paint to touch up small scratches? Do I use the same method like metal bodies (sanding etc.) How about repairing small dents?<p>2) When the car is at rest, the temperature gauge is at 100F. When the car starts, it jumps to the extreme left (way off the scale). Is this what they called the 'pegging' problem? What is the cause of it? It used to be working. Do I really have to mess with the display console to fix it?<p>3) The car oil pressure normally runs around 40-60. Is this normal?<p>4) The speedometer kind of vibrates. I can actually hear the vibration when the car moves. Is this normal? Tachometer does not vibrate at all.<p>5) Some strange smell coming from the engine. Not fuel and no smoke from engine as well. What could it be?<p><br>Many thanks.
Z||000001||DJRice||08-05-99||10:54 AM||||1) you cant get to crazy sanding it because you will sand through it but if you use good judgement you should be OK.<p>2) The temperature pegging problem is when your temp needle gets stuck off of the scale.<br>If your needle does not return to the 100F position after starting, that is the pegging problem.  The movement when you start the car is normal, although if Pontiac had decided not to apply power to the gauge until after the engine had started, the pegging problem would not exist.  There are several ways to fix the problem, search through earlier posts to see them (set your browser to view posts from last 100 days)<p>3)That oil pressure sounds about right.....<p>4)As I recall you mentioned in another thread that this car had been sitting for several years.  If you had been sitting for several years, you would smell funny too.  <p>How do you know its not fuel, the system could be running rich, but  I think that would trigger the "Service Engine" light.<p>You could also have tree leaves or something in the vicinity of things that get hot and those could be burning off.  Someone could have even spilled oil or something a while ago, but if the engine did not run, it couldnt burn off.
Z||000002||theogre||08-05-99||03:35 PM||||the pegging problem is actually the fact that the temp gauge swings up when starting. The end result is that it can damage the gauge, or at least get the needle stuck.<p>the problem is caused by the factory reversal of the wiring for the gauge, and the temp warning light. (You'll note that this light doesn't come on when starting.....)<p>Fixing the thing is fairly easy. The quick fix it to remove the light green wire from it's position in the ignition switch block. That solves the gauge. If you want to make the temperature warning light have it's lamp test function, then it's a bit more involved.<p>The speedo has some small motors in it. One of them could be messed up.
Z||000003||Rod101||08-05-99||10:05 PM||||Really!?? Just remove the light green wire and the gauge should work? I got to try that tomorrow. In that case shouldn't ALL those Fieros that has these bad connections have bad temp gauge? Why no one complain at that time when the problem started?
Z||000004||Rod101||08-05-99||10:25 PM||||Forgot some VERY important questions:<p>(or what I found out in a new car in 1 day)<p>1) There is some brown stuff on the radiator cap. It looks suspeciously like rust. Am I screwed? Can I just go one of those so called 'Professional strength' chemical radiator flush and live by it or I have to replace the whole cooling system? (i.e. radiator, hose, pipes etc. I hope not).<p>2) The car pulls left when I brake. That is a common braking system problem right?<p>3) Oil leak the size of a hand. Not too good I guess.<p>I am sure getting worried... then again, it is 11 year old car, with 100psi pressure in each cylinder ;) And I am surprise the car can mantain a 75 mph on a free way with minimal shake on the steering wheel.<br>
Z||000005||theogre||08-05-99||10:29 PM||||that's about it........<p>Most people don't understand that the pegging needle is an error at the plant. That's why it was never fixed by a recall. It's also not seen as a safety issue by the NTSB or NHTSA.<p>the wire can be released from the connector by slipping a small flat screw driver into the the space between the outside of the shell and the terminal. The screwdriver depresses the lock tab, and then the terminal comes out the back. Tape the terminal, tuck it out of the way, and the gauge won't peg any more.
Z||000006||Patrick||08-05-99||10:32 PM||||<br>I'll take a stab at #5.<p>If the "strange smell" is similar to rotten eggs, that would be from your catalytic converter. Mine smells bad until the cat gets good and hot, then it's fine. If the smell never goes away when the engine is running, your cat is shot. Replace it.<p><p>
Z||000007||theogre||08-05-99||10:45 PM||||Pat,<p>If the cat is producing sulfer smell, E.I. the rotten egg smell, then there is a problem. Likely the catalyst is poisoned. It should be replaced, but before it's replaced make sure there is no antifreeze or oil being burned. Either one will poison a cat almost as bad as using leaded gas.
Z||000008||Rod101||08-05-99||11:05 PM||||About the smell.<p>Actually I found out (appears to me) that it is the combination of coolant and motor oil on a hot metal. A little like 'after rain'. ;)<p>Would you believe it? The machanic actually MISSED the overfill coolant container. There is NO COOLENT AT ALL in the overfill container. None. I found out when I went sniffing all around.<p>And he missed the rotting overfill cap and radiator cap. Read the post above about rusting caps etc. Also there is coolant leak from the radiator cap. Seems to be caused by a crack at the rubber on the radiator cap. The leak cause the burn.<p>Tomorrow I will raise hell on his boss. I don't think this mechanic is up to it. (Fiero too old or too complicated?)<p>
Z||000009||Patrick||08-06-99||12:05 AM||||<br>Ogre, my 2.5L '87 Fiero has over 200,000K on it. I don't know if the engine has ever been overhauled or not. It DOES go through a little oil, not that I ever see blue smoke. The coolant level never changes. <p>The cat that is on it now was replaced just over a year ago, and the car sailed through the smog test a couple of months ago no problem. Prior to changing the cat last year, the emission levels were real bad. This would indicate to me that he cat is still working fine. I don't dispute what you say at all. It's just that even if the cat has been "poisoned" (by oil), it still appears to be doing its job.<p>
